EVERY LINE OF A CONTINUATION LANDS AT THE CONTENT INDENT, NOT ONLY THE FIRST.

The writer treats an item's first line differently from the rest: the first
carries the marker and the checkbox, the rest line up under the content. A
continuation has no marker, so ALL of its lines take the second path,
including the one that would have been first.

Get that wrong and only line one moves. `tail line two` would keep an indent
meant for a line that follows a marker, and the two halves of one soft wrapped
sentence would sit in different columns.

A soft wrap is also the shape that HIDES indent defects, which is why it is
worth its own case. A lazy continuation line is paragraph text whatever its
indentation, so cmark reads the paragraph back correctly even when the columns
are wrong, and a case that only round trips would see nothing. This one pins
the bytes.
